ngs. The hot-path goldens were re-pinned, so they pin bytes, not this behaviour. Also, the head moving right (kLevelX, _level_x at chart_svg.py:917) and the group-heading wrap (kWrapPx, _wrapped_px) on the kit side are verified only by the @pytest.mark.integration Chromium tests, which no CI job runs. Add static-drawer unit tests of the shape :157-171 uses: DRAWERS['scatter_frontier'] with a label placed across each reference line, and DRAWERS['bars'] with a note the reference line crosses. Also add a scatter and a noted bars chart to _page, so the kit side passes check.py too. Scope: instance.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/chart_svg.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/kit/kit.js", "docs/eval-report-kit/kit/kit.js", "packages/evals/tests/test_presentation_chart_clearance.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Raw stratum ids survive on display surfaces outside the one name: derived case labels on the written page and the print's stratum lines", "recommendation": "Item 9 asks for one spelling per stratum. fca62658f routes the aspect page's By stratum header, Cases column and thin-stratum note through _stratum_word (build.py:2877, 2895, 3313). Two surfaces still print the stored id. (1) bundle/trials.py:456 builds an undeclared case's label as f\"{stratum}: {lead}\" from the raw id. On the written page that label is printed unfiltered in the Examples table's Case column (parts/resolve_table.py:398), beside a Stratum column that reads the humanised name (:399), so one row reads 'off_lane: ...' next to 'off lane'. The same label appears in the world-state summary (serialize_html.py:581, serialize_md.py:248/265). (2) summary.py:1069 prints a run's stratum_lines as _shown(stratum.stratum), so the print and exit line read 'off_lane: 2 case(s)' while the page reads 'off lane'. That breaks the page-and-print one-name rule that test_presentation_one_reading.py:449 pins for judged dimensions. Stored ids, the JSON body and figure keys (strata.off_lane.*) rightly keep the raw id, and body:json goldens are unchanged. Scope: class. Search every f-string or join that interpolates a `.stratum`/stratum id outside reader_name/_stratum_word. Close the class with one owner, the reader name, for every printed stratum; a longer list of call sites will not close it.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/trials.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/resolve_table.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/summary.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "Result.strata() is keyed by the humanised column header: a host keying by stratum id breaks, and two ids that humanise alike silently merge", "recommendation": "quick/result.py:782-797 keys each row's `strata` dict by the By stratum column header. After fca62658f (build.py:2977) that header is the humanised id for an undeclared stratum. A quick-path caller who declared `stratum: \"off_lane\"` on its cases and reads row[\"strata\"][\"off_lane\"] now gets a KeyError. test_quick_strata.py uses only one-word strata ('lookalike', 'plain'), so nothing caught it. Humanising also adds a collision that assemble.py:1002-1011's clash guard does not cover, because it guards declared names only. Ids 'off_lane' and 'off.lane', or an undeclared 'off_lane' beside a stratum declared 'off lane', now print two columns with one header, and Result.strata's dict comprehension drops one of them silently. This is the owner's id-versus-name question: a program surface should keep the raw id. Key Result.strata by stratum id, and give the display name separately if hosts want it, or refuse or disambiguate a humanised collision as declared clashes are. Add a test with a multi-word stratum id. Note it in CHANGELOG, which this chunk does not touch. Scope: instance.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/quick/result