label through judged_label, with _row_label calling it rather than re-deriving it.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/words.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/harness/writer_eval.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "check.py's interval-wrap check sees only a whole-cell interval or a cell's first .iv span", "recommendation": "The new checks test the defect, not the implementation. 'crossed' (check.py:45-57) compares rendered boxes of axis or floor lines against text boxes. 'wrapped' (:215-223) counts the line tops of the interval's rendered range. The mark check reads the computed contrast. The negative integration test (test_presentation_chart_clearance.py:244-268) shows each check fires. The wrap check's reach is narrow, though. It reads `cell.querySelector('.iv') || cell` and skips any text that is not an interval end to end, so a second interval in one cell, or an interval inside a figure cell ('30% (0 to 67)', which _held_figures leaves unspanned), is never checked. 'crossed' deliberately excludes grid lines. Whether that matters is the builder's call. Scope: none.", "files": ["docs/eval-report-kit/scripts/check.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Contract text still says an unnamed stratum is printed as its id", "recommendation": "After fca62658f an unnamed stratum prints as its humanised id. These descriptions still say it prints as its id: the host-facing field descriptions at schema/models.py:1878-1880 (EvalTemplate.stratum_names) and bundle/schema.py:2917-2919, plus the _stratum_names docstring and both warning logs at bundle/assemble.py:981-986, 1000 and 1008. Make them relational ('printed by its reader name'), or delete the clause. Scope: instance.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/schema/models.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/schema.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/assemble.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A stratum's display name still has parallel owners: the fix restates figure_keys' rule in build._stratum_word instead of routing both pages through one function", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The residual (item 9: 'off lane' vs 'off_lane') happened because the written page and the code page each spelled the stratum rule for themselves. This commit makes the two agree by copying the written page's rule into the code page. It does not give the rule one owner. The rule 'declared name, else the id humanized' is now written out separately at build.py:2971-2977 (_stratum_word, read from bundle.stratum_names), at figure_keys.py:3538-3540 (reader_name 'stratum') and again at figure_keys.py:1954 (_stratum_field '.name'). The docstring at build.py:2972-2974 calls the result 'the one name ... (figure_keys.reader_name)', but nothing calls that function. The next change to how a stratum is named (for example a label capital, as judged rows just got) has to land in three places, or the two pages split again. Fix it structurally: one public function in words.py or style.py, stratum_name(id, declared: str | None), called by all three sites. Do the same for the identical aspect and task pairs (figure_keys.py:843 vs 3528, 1705 vs 3543), which have the same shape. Search for `or humanize(\"dimension\"` to bound the class.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A judged row's table label has more than one owner: build._row_label restates words.judged_label over a composed heading, and charting.measure_words names the same dimension from a different source", "recommendation": "Scope: class. words.py:291-304 adds judged_label as the label form, and the commit says it is the one owner. But the Contrasts, corrected-comparison statistics, guardrail and bar tables (build.py:2129, 2154, 2328, 2548, 2630) never call it. They go through a second private rule, build.py:1670-1674 _row_label, which capitalises an already-composed verdict.heading (judged_heading plus scale). Its docstring says it writes the label 'as judged_label writes it'. Only the readings, unasked and strata tables (build.py:1573, 2765, 3062) call judged_label. The charts name the same dimension through charting.py:378 (`report_input.judged[key].label or measure_name(key)`), which reads a declared Judged.label first. judged_label/judged_heading never read one (words.py:288, measure_name over the id). So the commit message and test_judged_labels_on_tables.py's docstring ('the dimension's display name') hold only because report_input_build never sets Judged.label today. Consequence: a change to the judged label in judged_label (or a declared label) reaches three tables and not the other four. Fix: make the label casing one function in words.py (as_label(text)) that judged_label, _row_label and measure_words all call. Then either pass the table rows judged_label(name) plus the scale suffix rather than recasing verdict.heading, or rename _row_label so it does not claim to be judged_label's twin. Decide whether a table's judged label honours a declared Judged.label, as the chart does, and pin that.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/words.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/charting.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A table cell's interval-ness is recovered by a regex over its printed text, and the check that guards the no-break rule uses the same regex, so a number form the regex misses loses the rule and the check together", "recommendation": "Scope: class. html_parts.py:764-771 (_INTERVAL_END/_INTERVAL_CELL/_INTERVAL_SPAN) and _words_class (html_parts.py:792-799) decide that a words cell is an interval by matching its printed text against `[+-\u2212]?$?digits[%\u00d7k]?`. _held_figures (html_parts.py:778-789) then wraps that text in an .iv span. The table model already knows which cells are intervals: every interval column is declared `TableColumn(key=\"interval\", term=\"interval\")` (build.py:2214, 2605, 2677; campaign/scorecard.py:822, 1446, 1685, 1853, 2118). The regex re-derives that fact and has to track every form the page prints. Durations ('340ms to 1.2s', the kit's dur format), an 'M' suffix and a unit between the ends do not match, and those cells silently keep breaking. check.py:218 copies the same pattern and skips any cell it does not match. So a missed form is invisible to the gate meant to catch it, which is the shared-blind-spot shape of R-7 (accepted, about the glossed level string). Fix: mark interval cells from structure, by the column's term/key in grid_html, or a flag on TableCell set where interval_text builds the string. Keep the regex only for the chart values table, whose rows are bare strings (_value_cell, html_parts.py:801). Have check.py find intervals by the .iv span or the column, not by re-matching the text.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/html_parts.py", "docs/eval-report-kit/scripts/check.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Descriptions still say an undeclared stratum is 'printed as its id', and the clash guard they underwrite no longer guarantees distinct names now that the fallback humanizes", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. After fca62658f, every table prints an undeclared stratum as its id humanized ('off lane'). These still say 'printed as its id': the host-facing EvalTemplate.stratum_names description (schema/models.py:1881), AnalysisContextBundle.stratum_names (analysis/bundle/schema.py:2918), and assemble._stratum_names' docstring and its two warning logs (analysis/bundle/assemble.py:983, 1000, 1008). The assemble docstring's promise 'so a page never prints two strata alike' relied on undeclared strata printing as unique ids. Humanizing breaks that: ids differing only by '_' or '.', or a declared name equal to another stratum's humanized id, now print alike. Nobody acts wrongly on the prose. Either delete the 'as its id' clauses or make them relational ('as the page names an undeclared stratum'). Either check the clash against the humanized names too, or drop the 'never alike' claim. Do not reword the history.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/schema/models.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/schema.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/bundle/assemble.py"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Shipped comments narrate history: a chunk name and a before-figure in src", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. html_parts.py:783-784 cites \"Discodon's C5b re-shoot\", a work-cycle name that resolves nowhere once the plan is archived. It is the only chunk-name citation in packages/evals/src. kit.js:216-217 (both identical copies, and the five rebuilt references that inline it) and chart_svg.py:1150-1151 say 'at .35 opacity it read 1.6:1 in light and 1.5:1 in dark', which describes what the code used to do. Delete those clauses and keep the rule (an interval in its colour at full strength reads at 3:1). The incident stays in the commit message and in test_presentation_chart_clearance.py's docstring, where it already is.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/html_parts.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/kit/kit.js", "docs/eval-report-kit/kit/kit.js", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/chart_svg.py"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "chart_svg's clearance twin adds _label_box beside _ref_box instead of reusing it as kit.js reuses kRefBox, with an always-True parameter and a width margin the head-room arithmetic beside it does not apply", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The clearance logic is one rule in two renderers. kVLine/_vline, kHLine/_hline, kGroupHeads/_group_heads, kGroupClear/_group_clear and kLevelX/_level_x match step for step, with the same 3 px pad, and the two kit.js copies are byte-identical. Python alone scales widths by _FALLBACK_MARGIN, which is deliberate: it estimates the fallback face rather than measuring. The divergences are small. (a) chart_svg.py:434-439 _label_box restates _ref_box (chart_svg.py:641-643): same box, plus an 8% margin and an `up` flag whose only call (chart_svg.py:932) passes True. kit.js's kHeadClear just calls kRefBox(rl, y-(n-1)*13). (b) _level_x (chart_svg.py:917-922) and the head's clear box widen the level head by _FALLBACK_MARGIN, but level_room beside them (chart_svg.py:1231, 1665) subtracts the unwidened width, so the margin label may sit inside the head's clear box. (c) _FALLBACK_MARGIN's comment (chart_svg.py:646-648) says it measures 'a strong label', but it now scales regular-weight text too (_text_box with strong=False, the level head, reference labels). (d) _dot_row's docstring first line (chart_svg.py:1146) is 143 characters against the 120 convention (E501 is not selected, so lint passes it). Reuse _ref_box (with the margin as a parameter) and use one width for the head in both places.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/chart_svg.py"], "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "kit/API.md documents the clearance rule only inside the zero_label row and omits the scatter's reference-line clearance, the group-heading wrap and the head's shift right", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. API.md:66 puts 'every vertical line ... stops short of the chart's head, a reference label and a group's heading' in the `zero_label` field row, though it applies whether or not a chart has a zero label. Someone reading the `reference`, `level` or `group` rows (API.md:57-58) or the scatter_frontier row will not find it. Three other behaviours appear in GENERATOR.md \u00a78 item 3 and in kit.js but not in API.md: a scatter's reference lines stopping short of placed point labels, a group heading wrapping at the chart's width (kGroupHeads/kWrapPx), and a level head moving right to start inside the chart (kLevelX). A bar's note is cleared only by the reference line, which API.md does not say either. Prose only, no wrong action follows. Move the rule into one place that every kind row points to, or delete it from the zero_label row and rely on GENERATOR \u00a78.", "files": ["docs/eval-report-kit/kit/API.md"], "fid": "R-16"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Host-visible changes in 5757aa3ae..332f57dd1 carry no CHANGELOG entry, and MIGRATIONS.toml:102's unreleased note now says the opposite of what the code page prints for an undeclared stratum", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the three commits record their decisions only in commit messages and test comments, and touch neither CHANGELOG.md nor packages/evals/MIGRATIONS.toml (git diff --stat 5757aa3ae 332f57dd1 -- CHANGELOG.md packages/evals/MIGRATIONS.toml packages/evals/docs is empty). The same class was accepted and fixed once already (prior R-10), and this range brings it back with new changes. The host-visible changes: (1) an undeclared stratum is now its id humanized ('off lane', not 'off_lane') in the aspect page's By stratum header and in the Cases table's stratum cell (build.py:2971-2980, used at build.py:2895 and build.py:3313). That cell is table data a serialized report carries, not only the HTML. (2) A judged row on the Contrasts, corrected-comparison statistics, guardrail, bar, unasked-readings, By stratum and All cases tables now starts with a capital ('Persona holds character (judged)'; words.py:291 judged_label, build.py:1670 _row_label). (3) The dot interval is drawn in full colour at 2 px, not at .35 opacity (chart_svg.py:1151, kit.js:217). (4) Vertical lines are drawn in pieces around labels, and the level head can move right (kit.js:144, chart_svg.py:442, chart_svg.py:917). (5) Interval cells gain iv/split/aside markup, and a td.num.split may now wrap (html_parts.py:109, html_parts.py:778). Consequences. Every Unreleased entry carries a Hosts: paragraph for this kind of change; see CHANGELOG.md:683-690, 'A host matching any of those strings ... updates its match'. Without one, a Discodon upgrade has no record that a stratum header or cell, or a judged row's case, changed. MIGRATIONS.toml:102 sits in the release being prepared (version = \"unreleased\", line 61), and release_notes.py prints from that file. It still states 'a stratum with no name prints as its id', and that a stratum whose declared name collides 'is left off the bundle and prints as its id'. Both are now false on the code page. The collision fallback existed to keep two strata distinct, and it now humanizes: ids that differ only by '_' or '.' (off_lane and off.lane) print under one header with no warning. The fix: add one Unreleased CHANGELOG entry covering items 4, 8 and 9 with a Hosts: paragraph, and correct MIGRATIONS.toml:102's two 'prints as its id' clauses to the humanized reader name, either noting the collision case or making the collision fallback keep the raw id.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "packages/evals/MIGRATIONS.toml", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/words.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/chart_svg.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/kit/kit.js", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/html_parts.py"], "fid": "R-17"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "check.py's new c